{"name":"Furious Tomato","hex":"#c30a12","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"furious","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Feeling great anger; raging; violent.","Rushing with impetuosity; moving with violence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/furious"},{"term":"tomato","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A widely cultivated plant, Solanum lycopersicum, having edible fruit.","The savory fruit of this plant, most often red when ripe, treated as a vegetable in horticulture and cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a shade of red, the colour of a ripe tomato."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tomato"}],"entries":[{"title":"Furious (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Furious is an American crime drama television series created by Elizabeth Meriwether and loosely inspired by the 1987 film Black Widow by Ronald Bass. Produced by 20th Television and Searchlight Television, the series stars Emmy Rossum as FBI agent Alice Black, alongside Lola Petticrew as a calculating serial killer Catherine Grace. The series also stars Quincy Tyler Bernstine and Scoot McNairy. It premiered on Hulu on July 27, 2026, and was renewed for a second season in August 2026."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/24/Furious_%28TV_series%29_poster.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Furious_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q140517950"},{"title":"Tomato","blocks":[{"text":"The tomato is a plant whose fruit is an edible berry that is eaten as a vegetable. The tomato is a member of the nightshade family that includes tobacco, potato, and chili peppers. It originated from western South America, and may have been domesticated there, in Mexico, or in Central America. The Spanish introduced tomatoes to Eurasia in the Columbian exchange in the 16th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/330px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomato","wikidata":"Q23501"},{"title":"Shades of red","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color red may differ in hue, chroma, lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a red or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Shades_of_Red.svg/330px-Shades_of_Red.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_red","wikidata":"Q7460345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg/500px-Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg/500px-Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solanum lycopersicum var. lycopersicum: one of the oldest surviving tomato specimens. Page from the En Tibi Herbarium, 1558. Naturalis Leiden.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANaturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Arrival_of_Cort%C3%A9s_in_Veracruz_and_Reception_by_Moctezuma%27s_Ambassadors_Painting.jpg/500px-Arrival_of_Cort%C3%A9s_in_Veracruz_and_Reception_